  "Number": 22,
  "Question": "In a far-off land three fish can be traded for two loaves of bread and a loaf of bread can be traded for four bags of rice. How many bags of rice is one fish worth?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{8}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Bread connects the two trades. Since each loaf is worth four bags of rice, two loaves are worth \\(2\\cdot4=8\\) bags. Thus three fish are worth eight bags of rice.\n\nDividing that value equally among the three fish gives\n\\[\n8\\div3=\\frac83=2\\frac23.\n\\]\nOne fish is worth \\(\\boxed{2\\frac23}\\) bags of rice, choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
